A||||7||wreakhavoc||Runs rough when engine is cold||||1||
Z||000000||wreakhavoc||09-01-2001||05:37 PM||||Lately whenever I start up my Fiero ('86 SE, V-6) it runs rough until it warms up- almost like it is misfiring on one cylinder.  After I drive it for 10 or 15 minutes it runs fine.<BR>Any ideas?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||k2m4||09-01-2001||05:52 PM||||I guess all i can say is join the club <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Kerm<BR>86 2M4<BR>86 1/2 GT||||reg||1
Z||000002||AL||09-01-2001||07:48 PM||||on a dark night, turn your car on, lift the deck and look for arching,that could be your problem.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Fformula88||09-02-2001||09:35 AM||||I don't think its all that abnormal for V6 Fieros.  I've seen lots of people who have had this problem to varying degrees and I have battled it for a few years on my Formula.  I have been able to cure it for the most part, but but haven't totally gotten rid of it.<P>Firsd look for any vacumn lines that are cracked or broken.  Fix as necessary.  Also possible the EGR tube is cracked, but that usually end up giving you a code 32 on the engine light.<P>Next get yourself some carburator cleaner.  Take of the intake tube and clean the thottle body out with it and a tooth brush.  If its gummed up a little bit it could be causing saome problems.  Start the car and run a bunch of the fluid thru the engine with it running too.  My mechanic suggested I do this and it helped more than anything else! <P>I also replaced my IAC valve, which may have helped a little.  Other than these steps, try a tune up... new plugs/wires/cap and rotor.  Maybe a new coil if yours is old.  <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||btoth||09-02-2001||09:46 AM||||My GT with the new engine even has problems until it warms up.  I just always figured that it had something to do with the computer taking control only when the engine reaches a certain temp.<P>When my engine is cold it idles at 1300 RPMs or around there.  If I don't first wait for them to settle back down to 900 before driving it, then I notice bad shifting and poor accerlation.  It only takes like 2 min of driving to get it back to normal though, so I just live with it.  I just don't try to push it till it's warmed up though.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||merlin||09-02-2001||10:38 AM||||I had a similar problem on my 87 GT and it turned out to be a bad coolant sensor, even though it wasn't throwing any codes.  The coolant sensor is a thermostatic resistor which means that it's resistance changes based on the temperature of the coolant.  A bad coolant sensor may be telling the ECM that the engine is warm when it is actually cold.  Therefore the timing and mixture will be set incorectly by the ECM because it 'thinks' the engine is hot, which could cause it to run rough until the engine warms up.<P>If you have a multimeter you may be able to test the resistance of the sensor when the engine is cold and when hot.  There should be a difference in resistance between the two readings.  Although, it is pretty tight trying to get the test leads onto the two prongs.  The coolant sensor is only about $20 and not too dificult to replace, so you may just want to try and replace it.<P>Good Luck   ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Formula88||09-02-2001||06:58 PM||||From what I've heard, most of the 2.8s are balky until they warm up.  Both mine and LZeitgeist's Formulas are like that.  His is a low mileage original and mine's been through the ringer (a few times) and they both run the same way.  Could be because engine management wasn't all that great back then.  Don't forget that even though the 2.8 was only rated for 140hp, that was a very high output for that size engine at the time.  There's bound to be a tradeoff in driveability when it's cold.  <P>Pity it doesn't run like the 3400SFI in my Grand Am.  That car can sit for a week - twist the key and drop it into drive and drive off like it's been running all day.  Ain't technology wonderful!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||Fformula88||09-03-2001||10:05 AM||||Other sensors you might want to check is the air charge sensor in the air box, and the MAP sensor.  I replaced both and the ECM coolant sensor without any luck on mine, but you might as well check them to be sure!<P>Formula88, this is a bit off topic, but I how do you like your Grand Am?  Is it an SE or GT?  Give us a quick review!  I've been lookin around for a new daily driver and its one of the cars on my list!  ||||reg||1
